// ConnectionStatus ONE VOCABULARY, used by every app that points at a connection: WORKING · EXPIRING · NOT
// WORKING · DISCONNECTED. Those four words are the whole set. `expiring` carries the date it
// stops, in `expires_at`; `not_working` carries the plain cause, in `not_working_cause`.
// `disconnected` means somebody here ended it.
//
// A CONNECTION ONPLO PAUSED READS `not_working`, with the cause "paused after ten failures in
// a row". There is no fifth word (Jonas, 2026-10-01). So does one waiting for a second
// person, with the cause "waiting for a second person's approval" — and `approval` says that
// in a form a program can read.
//
// STATUS IS READ, NEVER INVENTED. It is what the last check found, and `status_checked_at`
// says when that was — so a provider that could not be reached is shown as "from the last
// successful check", never as a green tick nobody can stand behind.
type ConnectionStatus string

// ConnectionStatusCounts How many connections read each of the four status words. Always all four. EACH NAME SAYS IT
// IS A COUNT (ONPLO-1743): `working: 3` could be read as a yes or a no; `working_count: 3` cannot.
// Until 2026-10-07 the four were named by the word alone.
