// CreateTunnelWithBody Set up a tunnel into the company's own network
//
// "A SYSTEM INSIDE THE COMPANY'S OWN NETWORK IS REACHED THROUGH A TUNNEL (ONPLO-5102, in the
// first release)." A database or an API behind the company's firewall either allowlists Onplo's
// fixed outbound IPs, "or the company sets up a tunnel and opens nothing". The company runs "a
// small tunnel program on a machine of its own"; it "calls out to Onplo over an encrypted
// connection on port 443, and keeps that connection open. Onplo sends each call down it."
//
// THE ANSWER CARRIES ITS FIRST JOIN CODE, ONCE. "Made by Onplo when the tunnel is set up, shown
// once, and pasted into the tunnel program on the company's side the first time it starts. It
// works once and expires after an hour unused." The program trades it for a credential of its
// own, "held as a write-only Keys entry of the kind Machine secret, so no person's key is ever
// on that machine". A further copy of the program joins with a new code, from
// `createTunnelJoinCode`.
//
// WHAT ONPLO MAY REACH THROUGH IT: "only the hosts and ports the tunnel lists". `may_reach` is
// sent whole and may be empty, "so nothing is reachable yet". "The program on the company's side
// may be started with a list of its own, and a call must be on both, so the company keeps the
// last word on its own machine."
//
// IT READS NOT WORKING UNTIL A COPY CONNECTS: "a tunnel with no copy connected reads NOT WORKING
// with the cause 'no tunnel program is connected', and so does every connection through it.
// Nothing falls back to a public address."
//
// A BUSINESS ENVIRONMENT'S ALONE: the catalogue offers the permission to a company and to
// Onplo's own environment, never in a personal one.
//
// NOT HERE: the admin's approval of a new copy on a device of their own (decision 1037), which
// no permission names yet, and reading the list of tunnels and their copies, for which the
// catalogue has no read.
//
// Setting one up lands in Activity. "An agent never sets one up or widens one."
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
