// knows only a URL, so the endpoint's address token is in the path, and it is the whole proof of
// which endpoint (the webhook endpoint's page, ONPLO-1844). An `Authorization` header sent anyway
// is not read.
//
// SIGNED THE WAY THE ENDPOINT'S SCHEME SAYS (`signature_scheme`, ONPLO-3738), over the exact bytes
// sent, with the endpoint's signing secret. While a rotation overlaps, either secret verifies, and
// the log names which one did.
//   - Onplo (Standard Webhooks): `webhook-id`, `webhook-timestamp` and `webhook-signature`, `v1,` and a
//     base64 HMAC-SHA256 of `<id>.<timestamp>.<body>`. The secret is shown as `whsec_…`.
//   - GitHub: `X-Hub-Signature-256`, `sha256=` and a hex HMAC-SHA256 of the body, and
//     `X-GitHub-Delivery`, its id. It has no timestamp.
//   - Stripe: `Stripe-Signature`, `t=<timestamp>,v1=` and a hex HMAC-SHA256 of `<timestamp>.<body>`.
//     Its id is the `id` of the event in the body.
//   - Shopify: `X-Shopify-Hmac-Sha256`, a base64 HMAC-SHA256 of the body, and
//     `X-Shopify-Webhook-Id`, its id.
//   - Linear: `Linear-Signature`, a hex HMAC-SHA256 of the body, and `Linear-Delivery`, its id. The
//     body's `webhookTimestamp` more than 1 minute from Onplo's clock is refused.
//   - Cloudflare: `cf-webhook-auth`, the secret itself, compared in constant time.
//   - Checkly: `x-checkly-signature`, a hex HMAC-SHA256 of the body. Its `x-checkly-timestamp` is not
//     signed, so it is not checked.
//   - Figma: the body's `passcode`, the secret itself, compared in constant time. Its `timestamp` is
//     not checked, because Figma sends a failed delivery again up to 3 hours later, unchanged.
//
// A timestamp more than 5 minutes from Onplo's clock is refused, where the scheme has one and says no
// other window. A delivery with no id of its sender's is refused too: a repeat could not be told from a
// new one. Cloudflare, Checkly and Figma send no id, so for them a body byte-equal to one accepted in
// the last 24 hours is the repeat. Cloudflare and Figma sign nothing, so for them the endpoint knows
// only that the sender holds the secret.
//
// ONE JSON OBJECT, sent as `application/json` or as a form, up to 5 MiB (the spec lane's ruling of
// 29 October 2026, ONPLO-3737). Nothing in the body is judged here but that: it is handed to the
// flow behind the endpoint as it arrived.
//
// A DELIVERY THAT VERIFIED IS ALWAYS ACCEPTED, and "the sender always gets a clean OK" (decision
// 0453). How it is answered is the flow's Respond (the webhook endpoint's page, "How the sender is
// answered", ONPLO-4649):
//   - immediately — and a delivery Flows held or refused, a repeat, and a run Flows says is not
//     there — 202 `{"outcome": "accepted"}`;
//   - when the flow ends, Integrations waits for the run, at most 25 seconds (`run_outputs.proto`):
//     a run that completed is 200 with its outputs, `{}` when it kept none; one that failed or was
//     cancelled is 200 `{"run": …, "outcome": "failed"}` or `"cancelled"`; one still going when the
//     wait ran out, or whose end Flows could not be asked, is 202 `{"run": …, "outcome": "still
//     going"}` (the spec lane's ruling of 11 November 2026, on ONPLO-4591).
//
// Never a 5xx for how a run ended, which would make the sender deliver it again. One that arrives
// while the flow is paused, undeployed or over quota is accepted too, and Flows holds it or refuses
// it; the endpoint's log says which, and how the sender was answered (`listWebhookDeliveries`). The
// same id of the sender's again within 24 hours is accepted and starts nothing a second time.
//
// BREAKING ON 11 NOVEMBER 2026 (ONPLO-4591): every accepted delivery was answered 204 with no body
// until then. The 204 is gone; 202 and 200 replace it.
//
// A REFUSAL IS LOGGED, with no payload: too large (413) or not verified (400,
// `webhook_signature_not_verified`). Refusals at one
// endpoint for the same reason within a minute share one line with a count.
//
// IT IS LIMITED BY HOW OFTEN IT IS CALLED, NEVER BY WHO CALLS (decision 0732). The server counts
// calls a minute from one address and from everyone together, and answers 429 past either, with
// the seconds to wait.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type.
//
// Corresponds with POST /webhook-deliveries/{webhook_address_token} (the `ReceiveWebhookDelivery` operationId).
func (c *Client) ReceiveWebhookDeliveryWithBody(ctx context.Context, webhookAddressToken string, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*http.Response, error) {
	req, err := NewReceiveWebhookDeliveryRequestWithBody(c.Server, webhookAddressToken, contentType, body)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	req = req.WithContext(ctx)
	if err := c.applyEditors(ctx, req, reqEditors); err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return c.Client.Do(req)
}

