// UpdateConnectionWithBodyWithResponse Rename a connection, hand it to another owner, change its sub-processor mark, or edit a Custom API's default headers and test request
//
// Every field is optional and an omitted field is untouched.
//
// A CUSTOM API'S THREE FREE FIELDS. "The name, the default headers and the test request are
// edited with Create & edit connections and no credential, because none of them is secret and
// none moves where the credential is sent." `default_headers` is sent whole and replaces what
// was there; `test_path` set to `null` takes the test request off. The headers are held to
// what connecting holds them to: each name once, whatever its case, and never the header the
// credential is sent in (422 naming the field). On a connection that is no Custom API either
// field is refused with `connection_kind_mismatch`, and nothing changed.
//
// NOT HERE, ON PURPOSE: a Custom API's base address, its sign-in choice, the header its key is
// sent in and its Basic username. Each says "where or how the credential is sent, so each is
// saved only together with the credential typed again, under its own permission" — so
// "somebody who may only rename a connection can therefore never point its key somewhere
// else" (decision 0775; ONPLO-2036).
//
// THE OWNER IS A PERSON IN THIS ACCOUNT ENVIRONMENT, and it matters who: a credential never
// outlives its person, so a connection is deleted with its owner when they leave the company
// (decision 0436). Naming somebody who is not here is refused (422 naming the field).
//
// AN OWNER THAT IS NAMED IS ASKED ABOUT, of the identity service, on every change that names
// one (decision 0670) — the owner the connection already has included, so a change naming
// somebody who has left is refused whoever manages it today. A change that names no owner
// asks nothing. A suspended member is a member. When the identity service cannot be asked,
// the answer is `identity_unavailable` and nothing changed: the same request can be sent
// again.
//
// A PERSONAL CONNECTION STAYS WITH ITS OWNER. Its credential sits with that person in the Keys
// app, and moving a key between owners is refused until that is specified (decision 0641). So
// naming another owner on a personal connection is refused with
// `personal_connection_stays_with_its_owner`, and the new owner connects anew — exactly as
// when somebody leaves. A company connection changes who manages it, and its credential stays
// where it is.
//
// A PAUSED OR A STOPPED CONNECTION IS NOT HANDED OVER (ONPLO-2663). Its credential never leaves
// its person, whatever its state: naming another owner is refused with `connection_paused` while
// its owner is suspended, so Resume finds it by them, and with `connection_stopped` once its
// company is closed. Every other field still changes.
//
// RENAMING A CONNECTION RENAMES ITS KEY. The entry in the Keys app is named after the
// connection, so the two never drift.
//
// THE CREDENTIAL IS NOT A FIELD HERE. Re-entering it and reconnecting each have their own
// operation, because each is a different act with a different permission.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PATCH /connections/{connection_id} (the `UpdateConnection` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) UpdateConnectionWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, connectionId ConnectionId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*UpdateConnectionResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.UpdateConnectionWithBody(ctx, connectionId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseUpdateConnectionResponse(rsp)
}

