// ApproveConnectionWithResponse Approve a connection somebody else made
//
// THE SECOND PERSON. Where a company asks for one on a new connection to a system holding
// personal data, the connection exists from the moment it is made and nothing may use it:
// it reads NOT WORKING with the cause "waiting for a second person's approval", and
// `approval` says who is waited on. This ends the wait.
//
// NEVER THE PERSON WHO MADE IT (decision 0633). The approver is somebody else whose role
// holds Approve a new connection — a permission like any other, never a named role. The
// maker is refused with `connection_approver_made_it`, whatever they hold.
//
// ONLY A CONNECTION THAT IS WAITING. One that is not is refused with
// `connection_not_waiting_for_approval`, so approving twice is said rather than swallowed.
//
// A PAUSED OR A STOPPED CONNECTION IS NOT WOKEN (ONPLO-2663). While its owner is suspended a
// connection reads NOT WORKING, "paused — its owner is suspended", and this is refused for
// everybody, a company admin included, with `connection_paused`: it would run or replace the
// credential of a person the company has suspended. Once its company is closed it reads "stopped —
// the company is closed", and this is refused with `connection_stopped`, because the grace period
// never reopens a company. Disconnect still ends either.
//
// A PERSON, NEVER AN AGENT, at any tier: approving is signing on somebody's behalf, and
// four-eyes means the eyes are human.
