// GetCustomApiAddressWithResponse Where a Custom API is sent, and how it signs in
//
// A Custom API's base URL, its sign-in choice and the header a key is sent in — "see a Custom
// API connection's address and how it signs in, never the credential itself", in the
// permission catalogue's words since decision 0811. No key, token or password is in the
// answer, and neither is a Basic username.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION, APART FROM READING THE CONNECTION. Reading is an act, and every act is
// its own permission: changing does not carry seeing, and seeing does not carry changing
// (decision 0811, rule 2). So `getConnection` and `listConnections` answer what every call
// carries and what Test asks for, and never where the credential is sent; somebody who may
// read a connection, or change its address, sees the address only when this permission is
// theirs as well.
//
// ONLY A CUSTOM API HAS AN ADDRESS OF ITS OWN: any other kind is refused with
// `connection_kind_mismatch` — a system from the catalogue is reached where the catalogue
// says. A disconnected Custom API still answers the address it had.
//
// A CONNECTION THE CALLER MAY NOT SEE answers 404 exactly as one that does not exist, as
// `getConnection` does.
//
// Returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with GET /connections/{connection_id}/custom-api-address (the `GetCustomApiAddress` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) GetCustomApiAddressWithResponse(ctx context.Context, connectionId ConnectionId, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*GetCustomApiAddressResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.GetCustomApiAddress(ctx, connectionId, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseGetCustomApiAddressResponse(rsp)
}

// ChangeCustomApiAddressWithBodyWithResponse Change a Custom API's address, or how it signs in
//
// "THE ADDRESS CAN CHANGE, AND THAT IS AN ACT OF ITS OWN. A new base URL, a different sign-in
// choice, another header name or another Basic username each say where or how the credential
// is sent. Each is saved only together with the credential typed again."
//
// THE WHOLE OF IT IS SENT, and replaces what was there: the base URL, the sign-in choice, and
// what that choice asks for — the secret among them, typed again even when only the address
// moved. Nothing of a credential is carried over from the old address.
//
// "THE CONNECTION STAYS, THE KEYS ENTRY IS NEW. Every flow and agent that uses the connection
// still points at it. The credential becomes a new Keys entry for the new address, and the
// old one goes to Keys' Trash with a row saying the connection's address changed." So
// `credential_key_id` moves — and is `null` when the new sign-in choice is `none`, which
// keeps no credential.
//
// AFTERWARDS IT READS NOT WORKING, "address changed, not tested yet", "until Test passes
// once": `status: not_working` with that cause, `status_checked_at` the moment of the change,
// and `expires_at` `null`. Entering the credential again does not lift it: nobody has checked
// the address.
// A connection waiting for a second person keeps reading so.
//
// ONLY A CUSTOM API HAS AN ADDRESS TO CHANGE: any other kind is refused with
// `connection_kind_mismatch`, and a disconnected one with `connection_disconnected`.
//
// A PAUSED OR A STOPPED CONNECTION IS NOT WOKEN (ONPLO-2663). While its owner is suspended a
// connection reads NOT WORKING, "paused — its owner is suspended", and this is refused for
// everybody, a company admin included, with `connection_paused`: it would run or replace the
// credential of a person the company has suspended. Once its company is closed it reads "stopped —
// the company is closed", and this is refused with `connection_stopped`, because the grace period
// never reopens a company. Disconnect still ends either.
//
// THE NAME, THE DEFAULT HEADERS AND THE TEST REQUEST ARE NOT HERE: they "are edited with
// Create & edit connections and no credential" (`updateConnection`). A default header the
// connection already sends that has the name of the header the new credential travels in is
// refused with `credential_header_is_a_default_header`: take it off first.
//
// NOTHING OF THE ADDRESS DIFFERS: refused with `custom_api_address_unchanged`. "Typing the
// credential again for the same address is the ordinary re-enter"
// (`replaceConnectionCredential`), which keeps the Keys entry. So this request sent again
// after it landed is answered that too: the address is already as asked.
//
// ITS OWN PERMISSION. "Somebody who may only rename a connection can therefore never point
// its key somewhere else", and neither can somebody who may only enter a key again. Changing
// the address does not carry seeing it (decision 0811): the answer is the connection, and
// the address it now has is read with `getCustomApiAddress`.
//
// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
//
// Corresponds with PUT /connections/{connection_id}/custom-api-address (the `ChangeCustomApiAddress` operationId).
func (c *ClientWithResponses) ChangeCustomApiAddressWithBodyWithResponse(ctx context.Context, connectionId ConnectionId, contentType string, body io.Reader, reqEditors ...RequestEditorFn) (*ChangeCustomApiAddressResponse, error) {
	rsp, err := c.ChangeCustomApiAddressWithBody(ctx, connectionId, contentType, body, reqEditors...)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	return ParseChangeCustomApiAddressResponse(rsp)
}

